; slack_bad_coeffs_leaves_indices_unmutated: a SLACK that faults on its
; coeffs register does not append to its indices register first.
;
; exec_slack must resolve both registers before appending anything. If it
; discriminated `indices`, ran the whole index loop, and only then
; discriminated `coeffs`, a bad coeffs register would leave indices
; already grown by an instruction that faulted. The observable difference
; is the vec's contents after the fault, which no vector can read, so what
; this pins is the fault identity plus the ordering that makes the
; mutation impossible.
;
; r0 is a real vec<int>, r1 is an int from calldata. Capacity is positive,
; so the append path is entered rather than the empty-capacity return.

VECI r0          ; r0 = empty vec<int>
PUSH 0
INPUT r1         ; r1 = 5, an int, not a vec<int>

PUSH 0           ; start_index
PUSH 3           ; capacity -- positive, two slack entries
SLACK r0 r1

PUSH 7
STOW r2
PUSH 0
OUTPUT r2
HALT
